Description:
    Everything `rails generate resource` writes, with the route drawn by
    `recourses` rather than `resources`. That one word is what hands the model
    its seven screens: index, new, create, edit, update and destroy, served by
    this gem without a controller or a view of your own.

    The controller it writes inherits RecoursesController rather than
    ApplicationController, so the actions and the views arrive with it.

    An attribute of type references gets a counter cache and both sides of the
    association: the migration adds an integer column to the parent table, the
    belongs_to is written with counter_cache: true, and the parent model gains
    the matching has_many, with dependent: :destroy since a child that cannot
    exist without its parent goes with it. The parent's has_many is written only
    where that model exists already, so generate the parent first. For the
    models an app already has, `rails generate recourse:counters` writes the
    same three pieces wherever one is missing.

    It also writes db/seeds/<resources>.rb, holding two rows: one with only the
    attributes the row cannot save without, and one with every optional attribute
    assigned as well. A row cannot save without whatever the migration marks
    null: false — write name:string! to mark one — nor without a references
    attribute, which belongs_to requires. Pass --no-seeds to write no seed file.

Example:
    bin/rails generate recourse contact name:string phone:string

    This will create:
        app/models/contact.rb, its migration, fixture and test
        app/controllers/contacts_controller.rb, inheriting RecoursesController
        app/helpers/contacts_helper.rb and app/views/contacts
        db/seeds/contacts.rb, and a line in db/seeds.rb that loads it
        recourses :contacts, in config/routes.rb

    bin/rails generate recourse admin/market name:string

    This will draw the route inside `namespace :admin`.
